<Wizard::IntroContent
  @isOptional={{true}}
  @imageAlt="Diagram showing a Vault client authenticating with Vault as an OIDC identity provider, which verifies identity with a trusted identity provider such as Okta, LDAP, or AWS, and returns a client token"
  @imageCaption="Vault as an OIDC identity provider enables client applications to leverage Vault when authenticating end-users."
  @imageSrc={{img-path "~/oidc-provider-intro.png"}}
>
  <:description>
    Implementing zero-trust architecture requires providing identity-based access to services within an organization. OpenID
    Connect (OIDC)
    <strong>allows clients to confirm their identity with Vault as an identity provider</strong>.
  </:description>
  <:features>
    <Wizard::IntroContent::Feature @icon="service">
      Using Vault as an OIDC provider allows external applications to receive verifiable identity authentication.
      Applications then use this information to make their own access decisions.
    </Wizard::IntroContent::Feature>
    <Wizard::IntroContent::Feature @icon="database">
      Vault as an OIDC provider is
      <strong>cluster-wide within a Vault instance</strong>, not namespace-limited by default.
    </Wizard::IntroContent::Feature>
    <Wizard::IntroContent::Feature @icon="org">
      A cluster first
      <strong>must define OIDC scopes to configure OIDC provider</strong>. Scopes should be minimal and client-specific, and
      must be defined in the CLI/API.
    </Wizard::IntroContent::Feature>
  </:features>
</Wizard::IntroContent>